<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Asclepius</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Asklepios, Aesculapius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Asclepius was the Greek god of medicine, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, whose symbol was a staff with a serpent coiled around it. His main sanctuary at Epidaurus attracted the sick, who slept in its halls hoping for healing dreams, and his cult spread across the Greek world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> adopted him as Aesculapius in the early 3rd century BCE, founding a temple on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a> Island.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/asclepius.jpg" alt="Asclepius" width="320" height="762" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yair Haklai · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asclepius-Rotunda_of_Altes_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
